{"model":"kimi-k3:cloud","prompt":"Author the complete public instruction schedule for one fresh coding project.\nYou are kimi-k3:cloud, the substantive data author. Output one complete JSON\nobject only, without Markdown fences or commentary. Think carefully before\nanswering, but keep the final object compact and complete.\n\nProject ID (JSON): \"staged-03\"\nBroad domain (JSON): \"document metadata processing\"\n\nLineage: fit-on none. Invent this project independently now. Do not reproduce a\nbenchmark, paper example, existing challenge or previous experiment. No concrete\nexamples or previous projects are supplied. Do not claim universal novelty.\n\nCreate three sequential coding rounds in one persistent module.py. The work must\nbe substantive pure-Python transformations with interacting conditions, unseen\ninputs and boundaries, not an arithmetic-only or instruction-copying quiz. Each\nround replaces exactly one existing synchronous one-argument top-level function;\nat least one target must be revisited. Use compact references in your planning:\nthe coding worker has 1024 output tokens, and actual reference code will later\nneed at least128 tokens of headroom including JSON escaping.\n\nReturn exactly these keys:\n{\"description\": string, \"lineage\": string, \"initial_source\": string,\n \"rounds\": [{\"source_texts\": [string,string,string,string],\n             \"target_symbol\": string}, ... exactly three rounds ...]}\nDo not output the schema notation itself. Fill every field with your own values.\nThe initial module contains only ordinary synchronous pure functions, each with\none positional argument. Helpers may call each other. No imports, decorators,\nannotations, nesting, file/network access or top-level computations. Optional\nmodule docstring is allowed. Every target exists in the initial module.\n\nEach of the four source texts per round is nonempty and at most640 UTF-8 bytes.\nThese become original user messages in their given order. IDs are assigned\nm01..m04, m06..m09, m11..m14. Fixed requests m05/m10/m15 follow each group:\n\"Update {symbol} in module.py to implement the latest changes described in our conversation while preserving all still-applicable behavior.\"\nThe assembler substitutes the target identifier. Do not author request texts.\nState all concrete changes in your source messages. Distribute requirements\nnaturally; include some ordinary context or clearly quoted non-authoritative\nmaterial. Do not write a manual recap, gold source selection or live-rule list.\n\nRequire maintaining earlier behavior while implementing new behavior each round.\nIntroduce an explicit replacement or retirement by round2 that still matters in\nround3, including in the final target. Specify cancellation scope clearly; a\nchange to one condition does not silently cancel independent earlier conditions\nor permissions. Round1 must also retain nontrivial initially specified behavior.\nRequirements must support new, retained and retirement behavioral tests with\nunambiguous accepted outputs and more than one valid implementation where feasible.\n\nThis call authors only the public schedule and initial code. Do not include tests,\nreference patches, expected outputs, a final mutant or private annotations. The\nschedule will be immutable when later calls author those artifacts. Ensure every\nround is specified, coherent with prior sources, and achievable by changing its\none named function while preserving other functions.\n","stream":false,"think":true}